BT /F1 11 Tf 50 720 Td 14 TL
/F1 14 Tf (Chapter 1: Process Management and CPU Scheduling) Tj /F1 11 Tf T*
(CPU scheduling is the process by which the operating system decides which process in the ready queue gets the CPU for execution.) Tj T*
(The primary objectives of CPU scheduling are:) Tj T*
(1. Maximum CPU utilization: Keeping the processor active as much as possible.) Tj T*
(2. High Throughput: Number of processes completed per unit time.) Tj T*
(3. Minimum Turnaround Time: Interval from process submission to completion.) Tj T*
(4. Minimum Waiting Time: Total time spent waiting in the ready queue.) Tj T*
(5. Minimum Response Time: Time taken from submission to first response.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Round Robin \(RR\) Scheduling:) Tj T*
(Round Robin is a preemptive scheduling algorithm where each process is assigned a fixed time slice called a time quantum \(typically 10 to 100 milliseconds\).) Tj T*
(If the process does not complete within its time quantum, it is preempted and placed at the back of the ready queue.) Tj T*
(Round Robin is particularly suited for time-sharing systems because it guarantees fairness and prevents starvation.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
