/F1 14 Tf (Chapter 3: Memory Management and Virtual Paging) Tj /F1 11 Tf T*
(Virtual memory is a storage allocation technique that provides an idealized abstraction of the storage resources that are actually available on a given machine.) Tj T*
(Paging is a memory management scheme that eliminates the need for contiguous allocation of physical memory.) Tj T*
(Physical memory is divided into fixed-size blocks called frames, and logical memory is divided into blocks of the same size called pages.) Tj T*
(The Page Table maps logical addresses \(page numbers\) to physical addresses \(frame numbers\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Translation Lookaside Buffer \(TLB\):) Tj T*
(The TLB is a high-speed hardware cache used to reduce the time taken to access memory locations in page tables.) Tj T*
(If a page number is found in the TLB \(TLB Hit\), the frame number is immediately available.) Tj T*
(If not found \(TLB Miss\), the page table in main memory must be accessed.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
